==============================================================================
1. THE INHERITANCE GRAPH — driftwood, as the estate really pins it
==============================================================================
    driftwood -> platform              implementations  @0.1.0
    driftwood -> platform -> nist      controls         @1.0.0
    driftwood -> nist                  controls         @1.0.0
    driftwood -> ico                   pricing          @1.0.0
    driftwood -> platform              threat           @v1
    driftwood -> platform -> nist      controls         @1.0.0

    nist is reached by 3 routes, at 1 version(s): directly, and through platform
    (twice, because platform is itself inherited for two different
    kinds). That is the diamond, and it is live today.
    ONE version across every route, so there is nothing to refuse.
    Section 5 splits it and shows what happens then.

==============================================================================
2. BASELINE COMPOSITION — what driftwood must satisfy
==============================================================================
    inherited baseline: ['nist-800-53:ac-6', 'nist-800-53:cm-6']

    A subclass inherits its parent's requirements and may add to them.
    It may never drop one. That is the abstract-method contract.

==============================================================================
3. COVERAGE — four real gaps, found by composing, not by review
==============================================================================
    REFUSED: 2 control(s) in the baseline have no live implementation
    REFUSED: 1 workload(s) pin a version the composed array no longer declares
      uncovered nist-800-53:ac-6: no policy claims this control at all
      uncovered nist-800-53:cm-6: no policy claims this control at all
      orphan driftwood-app pins 1.0.0

    All four are facts about the estate as it stands today:
    1. cm-6's only claimed evidence is 'require-policy-version'. No
       ValidatingPolicy of that name exists anywhere in the estate.
       The real guard is named policy-version-orphan-guard. The name
       appears only in the OSCAL map and in a hand-authored fixture
       PolicyReport, which is why the up-flow passes today.
    2. ac-6 claims may-run-root-if-attested, which is real, but it
       lives in policy/policies/ and the version array only ever
       reconciles distribution/policies/. Flux never installs it.
       ac-6 is still covered, by require-nonroot. The CLAIM is wider
       than the reality, which is why this is OVERCLAIMED, not
       uncovered. It would become uncovered the moment require-nonroot
       moved trees.
    3. LATENT, not live. The component definition writes control ids
       ['ac-6', 'cm-6'], and the
       catalogue writes them lowercase and UNPREFIXED ('ac-6').
       Two mismatches, not one: case AND the 'nist-800-53:' prefix.
       Nothing in the estate resolves one against the other today, so
       nothing is broken yet. It breaks the resolver this spike
       proposes. Be clear about what this spike does NOT do: it folds
       case against its OWN baseline in material/parties/nist.yaml,
       which was hand-authored in the prefixed form. The cure is
       untested against the real catalogue.
    4. Nothing in the estate declares a baseline. nist ships 1196
       controls. The estate implements two. The baseline in
       material/parties/nist.yaml is this spike's proposal, not a
       reading of an existing file. It is needed for the ac-6.10 case
       (a required control nothing claims). Gaps 1 and 2 need no
       baseline: a lint of the component definition against the
       policy trees finds both. Composition is not the only way.

